<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="9cd81e9b">&lt;p&gt;
    Data flow diagrams (DFD) are typically divided into a series of levels, the level 0 diagram also know as a context
    diagram shows a single process representing the system and how it interacts with external entities.  This can be
    further decomposed in additional levels as starting with the main processes at level 1 and then further breaking
    those down as needed.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Diagrams may be created from a logical or physical viewpoint.  Logical DFDs will show a generic set of processes
    representing a simplified view of the system without duplication as an idealized form of design.  The physical DFD
    will include things such as ownership, organizations, locations, cycle timing and the like either as the system is or
    will be implemented.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="122226f6"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="1c833593">&lt;em&gt;Valid vs. non-valid data flows: &lt;/em&gt; External entities are either people interacting with the system or sources
and sinks. Drawing a connection between two entities directly is not valid (non-valid flow). There needs to be a process
and a decision on how and why the data needs to move which is illustrated by the process and the flow. The following
relationships are considered valid: 
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Between a process and an entity (in either direction)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Between a process and a data store (in either directions)
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Between two processes which can only run simultaneously
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="3ef9d7f7"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="d488d7a1">To serve as a technology independent visual aid of how data flows within the current or future system.</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="1a4fa183"></attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="6d05689c"></attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="7351c8be"></attribute>
<attribute name="representationOptions" nodeId="a2542801">&lt;img alt=&quot;Data Flow Diagram (DFD)&quot; src=&quot;./../../core.i_t.common.base/workproducts/resources/dfd.gif&quot; width=&quot;556&quot; height=&quot;119&quot; /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;External Entity&lt;/strong&gt;: This is the source or the destination of data. Entities either provide data
        (sources) to the system or consume data (sink).  Other names used are: agents and terminators.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Process&lt;/strong&gt;:  This is the manipulation of the work that transforms the data. This can also be a
        “directed” flow, which is based on a business rule (or a logical combination of business rules).
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Data Store&lt;/strong&gt;:  The area where data is stored between processes for later retrieval by either
        the same process or another one.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        &lt;strong&gt;Data Flow&lt;/strong&gt;:  This is the movement of the data between the entity, the process and the data
        store and is illustrated with an arrow pointing in the direction of the movement.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="representation" nodeId="b15e1e8d"></attribute>
<attribute name="notation" nodeId="252d2d86"></attribute>
</Element>
